🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“DNS is not working on your production server. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately restart the server or change the DNS configuration.

First, I want to understand where the problem is.

I would start by checking whether the server can resolve a domain:

nslookup google.com

or:

dig google.com

Then I would check the server's DNS configuration:

cat /etc/resolv.conf

I would verify whether the configured DNS server is reachable.

For example:

ping <dns-server-ip>

If ICMP is blocked, I would use appropriate DNS/network checks instead of assuming the server is unreachable.

Next, I would test whether the problem is DNS resolution or general network connectivity.

ping 8.8.8.8

If I can reach the IP but cannot resolve google.com, that strongly points toward a DNS resolution problem.

I would then check:

• Is /etc/resolv.conf configured correctly?
• Is the DNS server reachable?
• Is port 53 accessible?
• Is there a recent DNS/configuration change?
• Is the issue affecting one server or multiple servers?
• Are there errors in DNS-related logs?

I would also check the DNS lookup path:

dig google.com

This helps me understand which DNS server responded and whether the query is timing out or returning an error.

If the issue is caused by an incorrect DNS configuration, I would fix it according to the organization's change/recovery procedure.

After making the change, I would test again:

nslookup google.com
dig google.com

Then I would verify that the affected application is working normally.

Finally, I would document the incident:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

DNS troubleshooting is not just about knowing nslookup or dig.

You need to understand:

DNS Configuration → Network Connectivity → DNS Server → Resolution → Application Impact
